<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">Twenty-fifth Dynasty of Egypt</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Twenty-Fifth Dynasty</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Twenty-fifth Dynasty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> was a line of Kushite kings from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nubia">Nubia</a> who ruled Egypt in the eighth and seventh centuries BCE. Under rulers such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piye">Piye</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taharqa">Taharqa</a> they united the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile-valley">Nile Valley</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napata">Napata</a> to the Delta and revived traditional Egyptian religion and art. They were driven out of Egypt by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assyrian-empire">Assyrians</a> but continued to rule Kush, as described in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kushite-empire">Kushite Empire</a> profile.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/twenty-fifth-dynasty-of-egypt.jpg" alt="Twenty-fifth Dynasty of Egypt" width="320" height="261" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Matthias Gehricke · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Rulers_of_Kush,_Kerma_Museum.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Twenty-fifth Dynasty, also called the Nubian Dynasty and the Napatans after their capital <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napata">Napata</a>, was the last dynasty of the Third Intermediate Period. It originated in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kushite-empire">Kingdom of Kush</a>, in present-day northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sudan">Sudan</a>, and most of its kings saw Napata as their spiritual homeland. The dynasty was highly Egyptianized, using the Egyptian language and writing and showing an unusual devotion to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>'s religious, artistic and literary traditions. Scholars have criticised the term Black Pharaohs, because it suggests that other dynasties did not share southern origins.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piye">Piye</a>, spelled Piankhi in older works, invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napata">Napata</a> and recorded his victory on the Stele of Victory, which names him <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pharaoh">Pharaoh</a> of all Egypt. Tradition holds that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shabaka">Shabaka</a> brought the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile-valley">Nile Valley</a> under Kushite rule, but recent archaeological evidence shows that he ruled after Shebitku, not before. Shabaka restored monuments and temples and moved the capital to Memphis.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taharqa">Taharqa</a>'s reign was prosperous, with a large <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile">Nile</a> flood and abundant crops. He gave gold to the temple of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amun">Amun</a> at Kawa, built or restored works at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jebel-barkal">Jebel Barkal</a>, Kawa, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qasr-ibrim">Qasr Ibrim</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karnak">Karnak</a>, and raised the largest pyramid in the Nubian region at Nuri. The imperial ambitions of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assyrian-empire">Assyria</a> made war inevitable. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/esarhaddon">Esarhaddon</a> invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> in 674 BCE and was defeated, according to Babylonian records, but in 671 BCE he conquered northern Egypt and captured Memphis. Ashurbanipal later advanced as far south as Thebes, and Taharqa retreated to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nubia">Nubia</a>, where he died in 664 BCE.</p><p>His successor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tantamani">Tantamani</a> sailed north from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napata">Napata</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thebes">Thebes</a>, where he was ritually installed as king, and reconquered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> as far north as Memphis. Psamtik I returned with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashurbanipal">Ashurbanipal</a>, Tantamani fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nubia">Nubia</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assyrian-empire">Assyrians</a> sacked Thebes in 663 BCE. Psamtik I became the first ruler of the Twenty-sixth Dynasty and in 656 BCE sent a large fleet south to Thebes, peacefully taking control of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-egypt">Upper Egypt</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>The dynasty ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> for less than a century, but it holds an important place in Egyptian history for its restoration of traditional Egyptian values, culture, art and architecture. It revived the building of pyramids for dead rulers, at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/el-kurru">El-Kurru</a> and Nuri, about 1,000 years after Egyptian burial methods had changed. Its successors continued their kingdom in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nubia">Nubia</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napata">Napata</a> and then at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meroe">Meroë</a>. Psamtik II destroyed the monuments of the Kushite kings in Egypt, erasing their names, and sent an army to Nubia in 592 BCE. Even so, the memory of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shabaka">Shabaka</a> survived, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egyptians">Egyptians</a> named a street after him in Late Period Memphis.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Twenty-fifth Dynasty of Egypt” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Nubia under New Kingdom administration was changed so thoroughly that the later Kushite kingdom centred on Napata adopted Egyptian gods, titles, and monumental forms, and would eventually conquer Egypt itself as the <b>Twenty-Fifth Dynasty</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/egyptian-empire-new-kingdom">Egyptian Empire New Kingdom · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Under Piye's successor Shabaka, Kushite rule was consolidated as the <b>Twenty-Fifth Dynasty</b> of Egypt, uniting Nubia and Egypt from the Mediterranean to beyond the Fourth Cataract.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kushite-empire">Kushite Empire · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
